While I agree that keeping good notes is not cheating, keeping object
identification written down IS, because the MMF attack is specifically
supposed to remove that, and circumventing that with out-of-game
knowledge isn't really appropriate.

Besides, since name-Y isn't affected by mindflayer attacks, the object
identification is simple to overcome in-game -- what I do is, when I
store stuff in  a cache, I name-Y it so that I don't mistake the
scrolls of charging for the scrolls of monster creation etc.  Then, if
I get hit by amnesia, I simply go back up and do a name-N on all the
stuff I had previously named-Y. Presto!  Object identification problem
solved.

I similarly name-Y all the critical stuff in my inventory as well so I
always remember which ring is the ring of conflict, teleport control,
etc.

The upshot is that there's no reason to write down object
identification OOC since the mindflayer attack effect can be
circumvented in-game using #name.

That's a backup defense, of course.  My major defense is not getting
amnesia at all.  In 11 ascensions I have not suffered a single case of
amnesia.  This was accomplished by the simple expedient of 1) never
reading an unidentified scroll worth more than about 90 gp (all the
scrolls I depend on cost less than that, and there's not a harmful
scroll in the bunch) and 2) never letting mindflayers into melee range
without "Elbereth".      I have not always been able to do that
perfectly, but a combination of low armor class, high luck, a good
helmet (greased whenever possible) and divine intervention kept me
from suffering a single hit.

